  <main><section class="article-hero section-pad"><a class="back-link" href="/#stories">← All research stories</a><p class="eyebrow">RESEARCH STORY · GREENLAND</p><h1>What happens before<br />an iceberg <em>breaks away?</em></h1><p class="article-deck">At Helheim Glacier, repeated high-resolution scans captured features forming on the ice surface long before some pieces calved into the fjord.</p><p class="article-byline">A plain-language introduction to research co-authored by Sarah F. Child.</p></section>
    <article class="article-body"><p class="article-lead">A calving iceberg is the final moment of a longer story. What clues are visible on the glacier before a piece breaks away?</p><p>At Helheim Glacier in southeast Greenland, researchers used autonomous laser scanners to repeatedly map the glacier’s surface. Alongside satellite imagery and elevation data, the scans helped the team follow surface depressions: broad dips and rifts that can develop upstream of the ice front.</p><h2>Watching the glacier between snapshots</h2><p>Satellite images are invaluable, but a glacier can change between satellite passes. The scanners produced a high-frequency record of elevations, point clouds, velocity, and strain rates, allowing the team to track features through time and compare calving events with glacier motion.</p><p>The study found that traced surface depressions originated in a small area on a subglacial ridge. It also reports that the grounding zone retreated by more than 3 km between 2018 and 2019. This zone marks the transition from grounded to floating ice, and tracking it helps researchers understand how the glacier interacts with its bed and the ocean.</p><aside class="article-callout">At Helheim, surface depressions accounted for most of the observed ice area lost in the study’s calving catalog.</aside><h2>What the observations tell us</h2><p>The high-frequency record helped the researchers test whether individual calving events were followed by sustained speed-ups. During the observation period, individual calving episodes did not trigger a lasting increase in glacier velocity. The study also found that variations in calving style did not change the velocity response.</p><p>Those results suggest that the glacier’s terminus may be less sensitive to individual calving events than previously expected. Sarah Child is a co-author of this work, which combines field instruments with remote sensing to study a dynamic part of the Greenland Ice Sheet.</p><a class="button button-dark" href="https://doi.org/10.1029/2024JF008059" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the full paper <span>↗</span></a><p class="citation">Shahin, M. G. et al. (2025). “Calving mechanisms inferred from observations of surface depressions at Helheim Glacier, Greenland.” <em>Journal of Geophysical Research: Earth Surface</em>, 130, e2024JF008059.</p></article>
